<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lescar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lescar is a town near Pau in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basses-pyrenees">Pyrénées-Atlantiques</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Usually identified with the Roman town of Beneharnum, which gave its name to Béarn, it was an early seat of the viscounts and the see of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Lescar until 1790. Its Romanesque cathedral holds the tombs of several kings and queens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> of the house of Albret.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lescar.jpg" alt="Lescar" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lembeye · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CatedralaLescar2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
